Chapter: E1000SwflagRefused
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 THE FALSIFIER FOR `k1-owned`. `e1000-swflag-k1` proves the guarded step takes
 the semaphore and lands its write; every row it prints reads the same way
 whether the acquire succeeded or was never checked, which is exactly how the
 result stayed unread from the day it was written until sitting 9.

 `-i219-mng-holds` leaves firmware holding MNG ownership, which is what the
 ASUS reads on the board: `extcnf 0x00F00=002c0089`, bit 7 set. The acquire
 cannot succeed there, and the step still runs -- bounded acquire, PROCEED on
 failure, which is the ruling and not an accident. What must not happen is the
 driver going in unguarded and nothing saying so.

 MDIO is refused in the same configuration, so the register reads -1 and the
 write cannot be shown to land or not land. That is the point: `k1-owned` is
 the one thing still answerable when the register is not.

Section: Fixtures

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  bring-up : PciScanResult -> E1000Device
  bring-up (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> e1000-init d
      is None -> e1000-absent

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in let d = bring-up scan
    in let step = e1000-k1-configure-guarded (d.e-mmio)
    in act
      print-line-uni ("shipped swflag step: " & yn e1000-pch-swflag-required)
      print-line-uni ("guarded owned      : " & yn (step.k1-owned))
      print-line-uni ("770.17 readable    : " & yn (step.k1-value >= 0))
      print-line-uni ("pch answer on init : " & show (d.e-pch))
    end
  end
